# Error Handling Guidelines

## Error Handling Strategy
- **Global Handler**: [src/lib/error-handler.ts](mdc:src/lib/error-handler.ts) - Centralized error handling
- **API Errors**: Handled automatically by API service
- **Component Errors**: Use try-catch blocks and error boundaries
- **User Feedback**: Display user-friendly error messages

## API Error Handling
- API service automatically handles HTTP errors
- 401 errors trigger token refresh
- Failed token refresh redirects to login
- Use try-catch for custom error handling

## Component Error Handling
```typescript
// Pattern for async operations
try {
  setLoading(true);
  const response = await apiService.getData();
  if (response.success) {
    setData(response.data);
  } else {
    setError(response.message || 'Failed to fetch data');
  }
} catch (error) {
  console.error('Error:', error);
  setError(error instanceof Error ? error.message : 'An error occurred');
} finally {
  setLoading(false);
}
```

## Error State Management
- Use local state for component errors
- Use Redux for global error state
- Clear errors when retrying operations
- Display errors in user-friendly format

## Error Messages
- Use clear, actionable error messages
- Avoid technical jargon for users
- Include relevant context
- Provide recovery suggestions when possible

## Error Boundaries
- Use React error boundaries for component tree errors
- Catch and display fallback UI
- Log errors for debugging
- Prevent full app crashes

## Validation Errors
- Use Zod for form validation
- Display field-level errors
- Show validation errors inline
- Prevent submission on validation errors

## Network Errors
- Handle network failures gracefully
- Show retry options
- Display offline indicators
- Cache data when possible

## Common Patterns
```typescript
// Loading and error states
const [data, setData] = useState(null);
const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);
const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);

// Error display
{error && (
  <div className="text-red-600 dark:text-red-400">
    {error}
  </div>
)}
```

## Logging
- Log errors to console for debugging
- Use console.error for errors
- Include relevant context in logs
- Don't log sensitive information

## User Feedback
- Use toast notifications for errors
- Display errors in modals for critical failures
- Show inline errors for form validation
- Provide clear error recovery paths
